## 1. Introduction
As counterparts to lithium ion batteries (LIBs), potassium ion batteries (PIBs) and sodium ion batteries (SIBs) with high safety and superior electrochemical performance have attracted much research interest as cost-effective options, because potassium (K) and sodium (Na) are natural abundance and more available in the earth crust compared to lithium (Li) [1–11]. Besides, K has similar chemical and physical properties to Li and Na as they are all in the alkaline metal groups [12–14]. Moreover, $\operatorname{K}^{+}$ (-2.93 V vs SHE) possess lower standard potential than other candidates, such as ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ (-2.71 V vs SHE), ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ $(-1.7~\mathrm{V}$ vs SHE),and $\mathsf{M g}^{2+}$ $_{-2.4\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs SHE) ions etc.[1,15–18], which enables PIBs to have a potentially higher voltage plateaus and higher energy density in full cells [19,20]. In this regard, PIBs appear as promising low-cost substitutes for LIBs in large-scale ESSs (such as electric vehicles, smart grids, etc.) [21–23]. However, the development of PIBs is being hindered by the shortage of appropriate host materials that can reversibly accommodate the repeat insertion/extraction of the large K ions (1.38 Å) [24–26]. In addition, the insertion of larger size ions $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{K}}^{+})$ into electrode materials are prone to cause a higher volume effect and sluggish kinetics during the repeated intercalation/extraction processes, which may cause structural collapse of the electrode and serious side reaction of electrolyte, thereby resulting in poor electrochemical reversibility and durability of batteries [27– 30]. Therefore, exploring high-performance anode materials for prompting PIBs systems is still challenging but urgently needed.
To this end, many efforts have been dedicated to seeking appropriate anode materials for PIBs, such as carbon-based materials [31–34], metal oxides/dichalcogenides [35–42], alloy materials [43–46], and organic materials [47,48]. Among the retrieved anodes, molybdenum dioxide $(\mathsf{M o O}_{2})$ , as a competitive anode material for the energy storage field, has recaptured scientific interests by virtue of its affordable cost, eco-friendliness, good electronic conductivity, and high theoretical specific capacity $(838\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}.$ ) [49–52]. Nonetheless, $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ electrode would often suffer from inferior reaction kinetics and dramatic volume expansions during charge/discharge process, leading to severe pulverization of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ and continuous formation of solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) layer, which undoubtedly discount the electrochemical performance and results in disappointing rate capability and short cycle life [53,54]. To settle the above shortcomings, one most pervasive approach is to hybridize active $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ with various carbonaceous materials, such as $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}/\mathsf{r G O}$ composites [52], $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ hollow nanospheres [55], and $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}@N.$ -doped carbon nanofibers [56], which can significantly improve the electrical conductivity and resisting the mechanical stress. For instance, Luo’s group have fabricated hollow $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}/\Gamma\mathsf{G O}$ sub-microsphere composites, which provide a high capacity of 104.2 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 500 cycles at $500\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ for PIBs [52]. Another efficacious strategy to improve reaction kinetics and cycling stability is to design an embedded structure with a porous framework that could prevent the agglomeration of active materials, accommodate the huge volume variation, leading to enhanced reaction kinetics [57]. Yu et al. designed and fabricated ultrafine Bi nanoparticles embedded in a three-dimensional conductive graphene framework for PIBs that realize superior rate capability and extremely long cycle stability [58]. In the case of promising $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ , Bao et al. prepared nanosized $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}\vert$ /three-dimensional porous carbon hybrids $(\mathsf{M o O}_{2}/3-$ DPC) via using $\mathsf{N a C l}$ as a template. It displayed high capacities of $463~\mathrm{{mAh}~g^{-1}}$ at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ and maintained $367~\mathrm{{mAh}~g^{-1}}$ after 200 cycles for SIBs, while it delivered a reversible specific capacity of $213\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $0.05\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles for PIBs [53]. However, the mixing of adscititious conductive carbon matrix with $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ often involves of a multi-step reaction process, which is not conducive to uniform distribution $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ in composite and thus decreases the electrochemical performance inevitably. Moreover, the underlying potassium storage mechanism of $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ has been rarely clarified.
Recently, metal organic framework (MOF) -derived metalbased nanocomposites (e.g., metal sulfides, oxides, and phosphides) have attracted much attention in energy storage devices [59–63]. The organic ligands of MOF could not only promote the embedding of active components into the carbon framework but also alleviate agglomeration of metal-based nanocomposites during the pyrolysis process [64,65]. For example, Xu et al. have recently reported ZnSe nanoparticles@nitrogen-doped hollow polyhedron composite derived from ZIF-8 for PIBs, which displayed superior rate capability and long-term cycle stability [66]. As a new class of MOF, polyoxometalate-based MOFs (POMOFs) has been utilized as a precursor to fabricate serious of Mo-based materials, such as $\mathsf{M o C}_{x}$ [67], MoN [68], and MoP [69], etc. However, little attention has been paid to explore $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ -based materials deriving from POMOFs toward PIBs. One can suspect that $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ nanoparticles confined within $N$ -doped carbon nano-octahedrons derived from POMOFs would be an ideal anode for PIBs by taking the following advantages: (1) The nanosized $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ caged in $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon nano-octahedrons can prevent the immediate contact of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ to the electrolyte, shorten the transport distance of ion/electron, and facilitate the kinetics insertion/extraction of $\operatorname{K}^{+}$ ions, thereby enhancing the specific capacity and cyclic stability. (2) The carbon nano-octahedrons with hierarchical porous structure can be helpful in facilitating the infiltration of the liquid electrolyte, providing a successive electronic transport pathway, alleviating unavoidable volume variations, and strengthening the structural stability of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ , which could enhance the rate capability and long cycling performance again; (3) the $N\mathrm{.}$ -doping could produce additional defects in carbon nano-octahedrons, which can improve the electronic conductivity and reactivity, thus displaying an enhanced rate performance.
In this regard, $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}@N.$ -doped carbon nano-octahedron $(\mathsf{M o O}_{2}@\mathsf{N P C})$ as a negative electrode was successfully designed and synthesized through a POMOFs-derived method. The resulting ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ is endowed with nanosized $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ nanocrystal embedded in $N\cdot$ -doped carbon nano-octahedron with hierarchical microand mesopores structures, which exhibits both superior cycling stability and rate capability for PIBs $(107.6\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $500\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ over 500 cycles, and $64\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ at $5000\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ). Intrigued by the high performance of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode, the underlying potassium storage mechanism of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode was analyzed by ex-situ technologies and electrochemical analysis. Moreover, the pseudocapacitive contribution of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode was characterised via a well-designed electrochemical method. Furthermore, density functional theory calculation and the galvanostatic intermittent titration test (GITT) jointly reveal that $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{K}}^{+}$ experiences a rapid migration process in ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ composite.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials synthesis
Materials and Chemicals: Copper(II) acetate monohydrate, LGlutamic acid, phosphomolybdic acid hydrate, trimesic acid, and iron chloride hexahydrate were of analytical grade and purchased from Aladdin Reagent Co. Ltd..
Synthesis of the MOF-based polyoxometalate $\mathrm{\vertCu_{2}(B T C)_{4/3}(H_{2^{-}}}$ $0)_{2}]_{6}[\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PM}\mathbf{o}_{12}\mathbf{O}_{40}]$ (NENU-5, BTC $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate) nano-octahedrons: Copper(II) acetate monohydrate $(4~\mathrm{~g~}),~\mathrm{~L-~}$ Glutamic acid $(3\mathrm{g})$ , and phosphomolybdic acid hydrate $(6\mathrm{~g~})$ were added to $800~\mathrm{{mL}}$ deionized water to form a clear solution. Then, trimesic acid $(2.8\ {\tt g})$ dissolved in $800~\mathrm{{mL}}$ ethanol was poured into the aqueous solution and kept stirring for $^{14\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Finally, the NENU-5 was obtained by centrifuge and drying at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . For comparison, Cu-based MOF $\mathrm{\vertCu_{3}(B T C)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{3}\vert}$ (HKUST-1) was fabricated with the same method without adding phosphomolybdic acid hydrate.
## 2.2. Material characterizations
The morphology was measured using transmission electron microscope (J Tecnai G2 20ST instrument) and field emission scanning electron microscopy (Nova Nano SEM 230 microscope). The structure was confirmed by X-ray diffraction patterns (XRD) with a RIGAKU instrument, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, ESCA LAB 250Xi), and Raman Spectrometer with high resolution (HR) evolution $\mathit{\Omega}_{\left\langle\lambda\right.}^{\prime}=\left.514.5\mathrm{\nm}\right.$ ). Thermogravimetric analysis was carried out using an STA 449C instrument from 25 to $700\mathrm{{\textperthousand}}$ . The porosity and surface area were characterized by BrunauerEmmett-Teller analyses (BET, Micromeritics ASAP 2460). The Cu elemental content was measured by inductive coupled plasma emission spectrometer (ICP-OES, Agilent 5110)Avio2001).
## 2.3. Electrochemical measurements
The electrode was fabricated by mixing the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nanooctahedrons $(70\%)$ , acetylene black $(20\%)$ , and polyethylene difluoroethylene $(10\%)$ in $N$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidinone to generate a uniform slurry, which was then casted on Cu foil and dried $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ The loading amount of active material is $1.0\pm0.2~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ . The 2032-type coin cells were assembled in an Ar-filled glove box where water/oxygen levels were below 1 ppm, with glass fiber membrane as the separator, K foil as the counter, and $1\mathrm{~M~KFSI}/$ DME as the electrolyte. The PTCDA electrode was prepared by spreading the uniform slurry mixed with the PTCDA $(80\%)$ , acetylene black $(10\%)$ , and polyethylene difluoroethylene $(10\%)$ in $N-$ methyl-2-pyrrolidinone on aluminum foil and dried overnight. The galvanostatic charge–discharge tests were conducted at Land Battery tester (Wuhan, China). The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and cyclic voltammetry (CV) were conducted by an electrochemical workstation (PARSTAT 2273).
## 2.4. Calculation details
All the simulations were performed using density functional theory (DFT) under Vienna ab initio simulation package (VASP). The ion–electron interactions were treated by projector augmented waves (PAW), and the function of Perdew, Burke and Ernzerhof (PBE) was used. For ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ , $7\times8\times5$ and $3\times4\times2$ Gamma centered $\mathbf{k}$ -point grids were chosen for DOS (density of states) calculation of K migration when cut-off energy of $450~\mathrm{eV}$ was applied. The threshold of convergence was set to $1\times10^{-5}$ eV and $0.01\ \mathrm{eV}/\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ for the self-consistent field (SCF) and ion steps, respectively.
## 3. Results and discussion
The ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ composite is synthesized by a MOF-derived strategy and the specific synthesis procedure is schematically illustrated in Fig. 1. Firstly, NENU-5 precursor is fabricated by a coprecipitation approach with copper acetate, L-glutamic acid, phosphomolybdic acid, and trimellitic acid as raw materials. As a kind of Keggin-type polyoxometalates, phosphomolybdic acid acts as a molybdenum source and occupied the cavities of polyhedral. For comparison, HKUST-1 without adding phosphomolybdic acid was also prepared in the same way as NENU-5. Figure S1 displayed the XRD patterns of NENU-5 and HKUST-1, which can confirm their favorable crystallinity and good phase purity. In addition, the color (insets of Figure S1) and the XRD patterns between HKUST-1 and NENU-5 show slight change, which suggests the successful introduction of phosphomolybdic acid in HKUST-1polyhedral. And then, the prepared NENU-5 octahedrons can be converted into $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}@\mathsf{N P C-C u}$ hybrids by directly annealing at $650~^{\circ}C$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize5h}$ under the argon flow, which is confirmed by the XRD pattern of $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}@\mathsf{N P C-C u}$ hybrids (Figure S2). Finally, the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ composite was obtained after being etched with $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ solution to remove metallic Cu by the following reaction: $2\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\mathrm{Cu}=2\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ $+\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ . The Cu elemental content of $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}@\mathsf{N P C-C u}$ and ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ measured by ICP-OES are $24.6\%$ and $0.02\%$ , respectively, which suggests the completely disappearance of Cu in ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ . The escape of gas (such as ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ during the pyrolysis process and the subsequent $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ solution treatment creat a large number of voids for the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ composite, which not only accommodate volume expansion during the discharging/ charging process, but also facilitate the infiltration of the electrolyte and decrease the diffusion path of potassium ions.
The morphology of as-obtained NENU-5 and ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nanooctahedrons was characterized with field-emission scanning electron microscopy and transmission electron microscopy. As shown in Fig. 2a and b, the NENU-5 particle displayed an octahedral sharp with a smooth surface, which is similar to that of HKUST-1 (Figure S3). After the multi-step treatments, the octahedral sharp was well preserved in the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedrons (Fig. 2c and d), while the surface is become rough and rich in nanopores, which suggests the porous structure of the obtained ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedrons. The TEM image shown in Fig. 2e further confirms the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedrons with rough surface and porous structure. The HRTEM image shows that the $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ nanoparticle with a diameter of $\sim10\mathrm{nm}$ was dispersed throughout the carbon, which allows efficient charge transfer between $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ nanoparticle and carbon, and accommodate volume expansion of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ nanoparticle during the discharging/charging process. In addition, a lattice spacing of $0.34\mathrm{nm}$ was measured for the embedded nanoparticle, which is coincident with the ( 111) diffraction of the $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ phase (JCPDS No. 32–0671) [70]. The SAED pattern (Fig. 2g) shows that the Debye  Scherrer rings are indexed to ${\big(}-111{\big)},{\big(}-211{\big)}$ , and $(-312)$ planes of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ . Moreover, the STEM image and corresponding element mapping results (Fig. 2h-l) of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron reveal that the molybdenum, oxygen, nitrogen, and carbon element were homogeneously distributed, indicating the highly dispersed of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ nanoparticles within the nitrogen-doped porous carbon octahedrons.
The crystallographic structures of all prepared samples are characterized by XRD analysis, as shown in Fig. 3a. The ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron shows the main characteristic peaks at $25.9^{\circ}$ ,
$36.9^{\circ}$ , and $53.4^{\circ}$ , indexing to ( 111), ( 211), and ( 312) planes of monoclinic $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ phase (JCPDS No. 32–0671)[70]. No other crystalline phases are detected, implying that the as-prepared ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron had good phase purity. Comparing to the bare $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ , the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron shows broadened diffraction peaks, which may stem from the uniform confinement of nano-crystalline $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ within the nitrogen-doped carbon porous nano-octahedron. Raman spectrum of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron (Fig. 3b) shows two broad peaks at around 1352 and $1584~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which is corresponded to the D-band and G-band of carbon [68], further confirming the presence of carbon in the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron. The $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ (intensity ratio of Dband and G-band) of 0.8 for ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron is almost equal to 0.79 for NPC, which imply that the introduction of phosphomolybdic acid hydrate has no effect on the graphitization degree of $N\cdot$ -doped carbon porous nano-octahedrons. Alternatively, the $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ of 0.8 suggests a high degree of graphitization of the porous octahedral carbon matrix, which is favorable to improve the electrical conductivity of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron. The unobvious characteristic peaks of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ for ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ was mainly ascribed to the thick carbon coating layer [51]. TGA test was conducted to determine the weight content of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ in as-prepared hybrids and the result is presented in Fig. 3c. The ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron experienced carbon combustion and conversion of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ to $\mathsf{M o o}_{3}$ during the entire heating process. According to the relative molar mass relationship between $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{M o O}_{3}$ , when issue that the content of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ is $\times$ , it can be calculated that the content of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ in ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron is $47.5\%$ based on the equation:1 - $(x{\cdot}144/128)=46.5\%$ The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherm curve of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron is presented in Fig. 3d, it displayed a type-IV isotherm with hysteresis loop at a high $\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}$ range, which suggests the existence of mesopores [71]. The surface area of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron was approximately $277.4~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ calculated by BET analysis, and the total pore volume was $0.39\mathrm{~m}^{3}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ . Moreover, the corresponding pore size distribution shown in the insert of Fig. 3d disclosed abundant micropores centered at $1\ \mathrm{nm}$ and wide maximum mesopores positioned at around $25~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which agrees well with the result of
TEM. The hierarchical porous structure was beneficial to facilitate the electrolyte penetration and relieve the volume variation of $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ , thus improving the electrochemical performance.
Furthermore, XPS analyses was carried out to better understanding the element constitutions and chemical state of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron. The XPS survey spectrum (Fig. 3e) demonstrates that the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron consisted of Mo, O, C, and N elements, which is consistent with element mapping image results (Fig. 2h-l). In the high-resolution spectrum of Mo 3d (Fig. 3f), there are two obvious peaks at $232.3\mathrm{~\eV}$ and $229.7\ \mathrm{eV},$ , which is corresponded to $\mathrm{Mo}^{4+}~3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Mo}^{4+}~3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ , respectively. Other two peaks at 235.6 eV and $231.8\ \mathrm{~eV}$ were ascribed to $\mathrm{Mo}^{6+}~3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Mo}^{6+}~3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ , respectively [52]. The appearance of ${\mathrm{Mo}}^{6+}$ in ${\mathrm{MoO}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NPC}}$ nano-octahedron arised form the oxidation of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ on the surface when exposed to the air [52,72]. The XPS spectrum of O1s shown in Fig. $3\mathbf{g}$ can be fitted by three individual peaks. The strongest peak at around $529.8{\mathrm{~eV}}$ corresponds to the Mo-O bonds, whereas the other two relatively weak peaks at 530.7 and 532.4 eV can be appointed to the CAOAMo and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bonds [73,74]. The existence of CAOAMo bond is further supported by the C 1 s spectrum (Fig. 3h) [73]. The intimate contact between the $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ nanoparticles and the nitrogendoped carbon porous nano-octahedrons with the ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}0{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{M}}0$ band is beneficial to electron transfer and conducive to its structural integrity. In addition, the high-resolution N 1 s spectrum (Fig. 3i) is split into three peaks centered at 397.8 eV, 399.6 eV, and $401.2\ \mathrm{eV},$ , which are assigned to pyridine nitrogen (N-6), pyrrole nitrogen (N-5), and graphitized nitrogen (N-Q), respectively. This implies that nitrogen element was introduced to carbon porous nano-octahedron.
The electrochemical properties of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode were evaluated by 2032 coin-type cells. Fig. 4a depicts the first five cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode at $0.1\mathrm{mV}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ with a voltage range of $0.01\sim3.0\mathrm{V}\left(\mathrm{vs}\mathrm{K}^{+}/\mathrm{K}\right)$ . In the first potassiation process, a distinct broad peak located at about $0.97{\mathrm{~V~}}$ without an appearance in the subsequent cycles can be ascribed to the inevitable decomposition of electrolyte and the generation of stable and compact solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) layer on the surface of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode for the first cycle [75,76], similar phenomenon has also been found in the CV curves of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ electrode (Figure S4). Moreover, a weak cathodic peak around $_{0.01\mathrm{~V~}}$ during the discharging processes can be related to the deposition of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{K}}^{+}$ ions [52]. Subsequently, an anodic peak at about $1.31~\mathrm{V}$ was observed during the anodic scan, which is attributed to the corresponding reversible K ions extraction from the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode. In the following scans, the positions and shapes for anodic and cathodic peaks are almost overlapped, indicating the high electrochemical reversibility of $\operatorname{K}^{+}$ insertion/extraction from the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode during the discharging/charging processes [75].
Fig. 4b shows the charge–discharge voltage profiles of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode. The voltage plateaus and slope shapes in the curves agree well with the redox behavior in CV profiles. The first discharge capacity of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode is $881.6~\mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , with an initial Coulombic efficiency (ICE) of $31.5\%$ , which is lower than $51.6\%$ for $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}/\mathsf{r G O}$ composite [52] and $44.9\%$ for the $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}/3\mathsf{D P C}$ composite [53]. Such a low ICE is mainly attributed to the reductive decomposition of electrolyte, the formation of stable and compact SEI layer on the surface of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode, and the irreversible reaction between potassium ions and the residual functional groups in the carbon nano-octahedron [37,77], which is well consistent with the rusults of CV curves (Fig. 4a). After the initial capacity loss, the charge/discharge profiles in the following cycles almost coincide with each other, further verifying the high electrochemical reversibility of $\operatorname{K}^{+}$ insertion/extraction from the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode. As presented in Fig. 4c, the discharge capacity of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode remained at 214.3 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles, far exceeding $51.9\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ for the $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ electrode and $49.4\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for NPC electrode, respectively. The improved potassium ion storage behavior of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode is ascribed to the synergy effect between nanoparticle level of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ and porous nitrogen-doped carbon octahedron. Based on the weight content $(52.5\%)$ of NPC in ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ (Fig. 3c), NPC contributed about $12.1\%$ of the whole capacity of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ . The rate capacities of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode were further demonstrated at different current densities from 50 to $5000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 4d), and charge/discharge curves were displayed in Figure S5. ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode delivers reversible capacities of 332.8, 278.2, 244.3, 203.9, 165.1, 120.3, and 64 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 50, 100, 200, 500, 1000, 2000, and $5000\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ , respectively. When the current rate reduced back to $50\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ , the capacity of was recovered to $272.2\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and remain almost stable from 70th cycle to 100th cycle, indicating superior rate performance of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode. However, the bare $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ and NPC electrodes exhibit lower specific capacities than the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode at the same current densities. The above results clearly reveal that the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode exhibits superior rate capability and high reversible capacity, which is mainly attributed to the chemical interfacial interactions and confinement of $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ within $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon porous nano-octahedrons toward fast electrons and K ions transport, as well as the robust structure. Moreover, the long cycling performance of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode was further investigated at a high current density of $500\ \mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ and shown in Fig. 4e. The ${\mathrm{MoO}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NPC}}$ electrode retains a reversible capacity of $107.6~\mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ over 500 cycles, indicating excellent electrochemical stability. Impressively, the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode exhibit one of the most outstanding potassium storage behaviors of the $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ anode and other anodes for PIBs (Table S1) up to now.
To investigate the enhanced electrochemical performance, the potential responses of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode and $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ electrode are measured by the galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT) test. As shown in Fig. 5a, the discharge/ charge curves of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode show less significant fluctuation than the $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ electrode, implying ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode has lower overpotential resulted by fast reaction kinetics[78]. Fig. 5b shows the variation of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ diffusion coefficients $(\mathsf{D}_{\mathsf{K}}^{+})$ derived from the GITT curves during the potassiation and depotassiation processes (see Supporting Information for the detailed calculation). Clearly, the values of the $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ electrode are much smaller than theM $\Omega_{ Ḋ }00_{2}@\mathsf{N P C}$ electrode, suggesting the fast potassium ions transport in the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode. Moreover, Fig. 5c illustrates the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) testing of $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ , NPC, and ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrodes in the first cycle. The largest semicircle of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ electrode indicates a higher interfacial charge transfer resistance than NPC and ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode. This may be ascribed to the confinement of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ within $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon porous nano-octahedrons and the ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}0{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{M}}0$ bonds which accelerates ion/electron transport during potassiation and depotassiation processes.
Cyclic voltammetry measurements in the range of $0.1{-}10\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ were carried out to further understand the kinetics of both electrodes. The CV curves of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode in Fig. 5d showed similar shapes with each other, and the electrode polarization gradually increases with the increasing scanning rate. Figure S7 displays the relationship between the peak current and the scan rate, the calculated b value of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode at cathodic and anodic peaks based on Equation S3 is 0.78 and 0.68, respectively, implying that the dynamic is governed by capacitivecontrolled process [79]. According to the Equation S4, the capacitive charge contribution can be further quantitative distinguished [80,81]. As shown in Fig. 5e, the percentage of capacitive contribution (blue shadow region) at $1.0\:\mathrm{mV}\:s^{-1}$ is $32.9\%$ . With the increase of scan rate from 0.1 to $10\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ , the capacitive fraction are $18.2\%$ , $21.5\%$ , $26.4\%$ , $32.9\%$ , $41.1\%$ , $60.3\%$ , and $82.4\%$ at 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0 and $10\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure S9). Meanwhile, the capacitive contribution of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode is larger than $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ electrode at each scan rate generally (Fig. 5f), which benefits the superior kinetics of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode during cycling. In addition, the density functional theory (DFT) calculations were used to deepen the understanding of electrochemical measurements [82]. Fig. 5g shows the potassium atom binding energy for bare $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ , nitrogen-doped carbon, and ${\mathrm{MoO}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NPC}}$ structural models. It is noticeable that ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ possessed a higher K adsorption energy than bare $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ and nitrogen-doped carbon structure, suggesting that K atoms are more readily adsorbed on ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ , which is consistent with the results of battery testing. Moreover, the density of states (DOS) calculations was further performed to enquire about the conductivity for both $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ composite (Fig. 5h). It is clearly observed that the valence band of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ significantly shifts to the conductive region with the addition of nitrogen-doped carbon, which further confirms the result of EIS analysis and means higher conductivity of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ composite[83].
To reveal the potassium ion storage mechanism during cycling, the XRD patterns and HRTEM images after cycled were conducted. Fig. 6a shows the ex-situ XRD patterns of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode at various states. The three main peaks of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ almost remain the same, which indicated the crystal structure of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ does not change after potassiation and depotassiation. The result was further evidenced by in-situ XRD measurement as well (Fig. 6b). Fig. 6c and d display the HRTEM images of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode at fully discharged state and charged state. It is clearly shown that the lattice fringes of the ( 1 1 1) plane from $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ grains have little change, demonstrating the $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ phase remains monoclinic structure, which is consistent with results of ex-situ and in-situ XRD patterns. The aforementioned CV, in-situ XRD, ex-situ XRD and TEM results reveals that the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode follows an intercalation pseudocapacitive mechanism for K-ions storage. In addition, the SEM images (Fig. 6e and f) and TEM images (Fig. 6g and h) of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode after 50 cycles show that the nanooctahedron shape is still retained after repeated cycling, highlighting the structural integrity and flexibility of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ electrode.
The excellent electrochemical behavior of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nanooctahedron electrode is ascribed to the following main factors. First, the confinement of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ in the carbon matrix prohibited the detachment or aggregation of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ nanoparticles during cycling. Second, the nitrogen-doped carbon nano-octahedron not only provides paths for rapid ion/electrons transfer but also accommodates the volume change during the electrochemical reactions. Third, the hierarchical porous structure facilitates electrolyte infiltration and boosts the mass and charge transfer during potassiation and depotassiation processes. Finally, the intimate contact between the $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ nanoparticles and carbon matrix with the CAOAMo band is beneficial to electron transfer and conducive to its structural integrity.
Considering the superior K-ions storage performance of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedron in the half-cells, a $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}@\mathsf{N P C}//$ PTCDA full cell coupled with perylenetetracarboxylic dianhydride (PTCDA) cathode and ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ anode with capacity ratio of 1:0.8 was fabricated and investigated further as illustrated in Fig. 7a. The electrochemical performance of PTCDA was first evaluated in PTCDA-K half-cell and displayed in Figure S11. PTCDA cathode delivers a capacity of $120\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $100\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles in the voltage range of $1.5\substack{-3.5\mathrm{V}}$ (vs $\operatorname{K}^{+}/\operatorname{K})$ . The voltage window of the full cell was set at $0.5\substack{-3.5\mathrm{~V~}}$ and the galvanostatic voltage–capacity profiles were presented in Figure S12 and S13. As shown in Fig. 7b, it delivers discharge capacities of 256.8, 131.9, 102.8, and $70.2\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 50, 100, 200, and $500\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. In addition, the full cell exhibits good cycling stability with $72.6\:\mathrm{mAh}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ retained at $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ over 200 cycles (Fig. 7c). The $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}@\mathsf{N P C}//\mathsf{P T C D A}$ full cell has longer cycling life compared with the reported other full batteries [15,33,37,84]. The energy density of the $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}@\mathsf{N P C}_{I}$ /PTCDA full cell is 125.5 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ . Encouragingly, the $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}@\mathsf{N P C}/$ /PTCDA full cell can easily power the LED light, as illustrated in Fig. 7d.
## 4. Summary and conclusions
In summary, a composite of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ nanoparticles confined within $N$ -doped carbon porous nano-octahedrons with ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}0{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{M}}0$ bond is designed and fabricated as anode material for PIBs through a MOF-derived strategy. This unique nano-octahedrons framework can accommodate the volume change, facilitates electron transport, and provide $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{K}}^{+}$ diffusion path during repeated potassiation and depotassiation processes, which are proved by DFT calculations and GITT tests. As a result, the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nanooctahedrons exhibit a stable reversible capacity of 107.6 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $500\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ over 500 cycles and maintain a capacity of 64 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ even at a high current density of $5000\ \mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ . CV, in-situ XRD, ex-situ XRD and TEM reveals an intercalation pseudocapacitive mechanism of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ nano-octahedrons. Additionally, full PIBs assembled by PTCDA cathode and ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N P C}}$ anode exhibits good cycling stability with $72.6\:\mathrm{\mAh}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ retained at $100\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ over 200 cycles. Our work present here provides an effective and viable structural engineering strategy for designing high performance metal oxide anode materials for PIBs and beyond.
